Bouncing ball on the Type 340 display, gravity + inelastic floor bounce, random start point

// Each bounce chain starts from a random point on either the top edge or the left edge of the
// screen, launches sideways at a constant speed, and falls under a constant downward
// acceleration (gravity). Every floor bounce reflects the vertical velocity and removes 1/4 of
// its magnitude (a simple, cheap restitution model -- see "Why shift-based restitution, not
// mul" below). A chain ends -- and a fresh random start point (and gravity reading) is chosen
// -- either when the ball has bounced down to a near-standstill or when it has drifted off the
// right edge of the screen.
//
// DISPLAY LIST TECHNIQUE
// -----------------------
// This follows cube2.am1's approach, not cube.am1's: position is computed live, every frame, on
// the host CPU (there is no fixed repeating cycle to precompute offline, since the start point
// and therefore the whole bounce sequence are randomized), and only the ball's y()/x() point
// words are patched into the resident display list before each dla -- exactly the same
// technique cube2.am1 uses for its lightpen drag target (tgty/tgtx).
//
// THE BALL SHAPE
// ---------------
// Neither the Type 30 nor the Type 340 can draw a circle natively, so, like every existing demo
// in this repo, the ball is a polygon traced in increment mode -- generated offline by
// FunStuff/Demos/gen_circle.py, not hand-picked. The very first version reused the tiny 8-step
// box from type340demo.am1's lightpen target verbatim (a placeholder). The second version used a
// single-octant midpoint/Bresenham-circle algorithm mirrored through 8-fold symmetry -- more
// accurate per-vertex, but for a radius this size it produces long runs (5-8 in a row) of the
// same repeated diagonal step near each 45-degree point, and each such run is a dead-straight
// chord that reads as a visible flat facet -- "cube like", as observed. This version instead
// samples the true circle at high angular density and keeps only the points where the rounded
// grid position actually changes, so the walk is always free to zigzag onto whichever of the 8
// directions is locally best rather than getting stuck repeating one diagonal for a whole
// stretch. Worst-case deviation from the true circle (gen_circle.py prints this) is about 0.2
// raster units at radius 8 -- under half a screen pixel at scale(1) (2 px/raster-unit), i.e.
// within the display's own pixel resolution for a shape this size, which is as close to "a true
// circle" as this size can get on this hardware. It costs more words than the octant version
// (16 vs 10 for the boundary) but is still well under half of type340demo.am1's 48-word sine
// wave, and there's no shortage of cycles for a single small sprite redrawn once a frame.
//
// The shape is: an invisible straight walk from the center point out to the circle's rightmost
// point (BALLRADIUS incright steps), then the boundary trace, all visible, back to that same
// point, then `end`.
//
// Since the ball now has a real radius, the floor logic needed a small adjustment to match: the
// ball's *center* used to rest at FLOORY (the same height the floor line is drawn at), which
// meant the ball would visually sink into the floor by its own radius once it was big enough to
// notice. RESTY (= FLOORY + BALLRADIUS) is the height the center now rests/bounces at, so the
// ball's bottom edge just touches the floor line instead of overlapping it. The floor line
// itself is still drawn at plain FLOORY.
//
// WHY NOT BARE x/y AS VARIABLE NAMES
// ------------------------------------
// TYPE340/type340defs.ah #defines x(a) and y(a) as the point-command coordinate-field macros.
// Naming the ball's own state variables "x"/"y" would be legal am1 (bare identifiers not
// followed by "(" aren't macro-expanded) but is exactly the kind of trap that's easy to trip
// over later if either variable is ever referenced from inside a parenthesized context. Every
// existing Type 340 demo in this repo (cube.am1, cube2.am1) avoids bare x/y for this same
// reason (progx/progy, tgtx/tgty, curhitx/curhity, sxtab/sytab) and this file follows suit:
// ball state is bx/by/bvx/bvy, and the two patched display-list words are ballx/bally.
//
// WHY SHIFT-BASED RESTITUTION, NOT mul
// --------------------------------------
// cube2.am1 uses mul against Q1.17 fixed-point fractions for its rotation-matrix damping, and
// that convention would work here too (bvy = mul(bvy, restitution-fraction)), but it requires
// prescaling/descaling discipline that isn't needed for a single scalar velocity. A bounce that
// keeps 3/4 of the incoming speed is just "bvy -= bvy>>2" (arithmetic shift, no multiply, no
// fixed-point convention to keep straight) -- the same leaky-integrator-style shift-and-subtract
// technique circle.mac itself uses for its curve, just applied once per bounce instead of once
// per point.
//
// WHY NO SUBROUTINES (no jsp/jda anywhere in this file)
// ---------------------------------------------------------
// jsp and jda were misused in earlier work in this repo (see cube.am1/cube2.am1 history) and
// they are easy to get backwards: jsp jumps straight to a body with NO reserved deposit word;
// jda deposits AC at a reserved "label, 0" word and jumps one word past it. Getting the entry
// shape wrong either walks straight into a HLT word (0 is opcode 0 = hlt) or clobbers the first
// real instruction of the body. This program's control flow -- gravity, position integration,
// a floor-bounce check, an off-screen check, a two-way random start-edge choice -- is entirely
// expressible as straight-line code plus the standard skip+jmp idiom (see "Skip instruction
// direction rule" in the am1 skill's examples.md: think about when the jmp fires, i.e. the
// no-skip/"don't branch" case), so no subroutine calls are used at all. That sidesteps the
// jsp/jda entry-shape question entirely rather than risking getting it wrong again.
//
// RANDOM START POINT
// --------------------
// First version of this file seeded from the BBN timesharing clock (rck, IOT 32). That turned
// out to be broken: IOT_32.c's millisecond counter only advances once IOT 2032 (cls) has been
// issued to set its enable bit -- without that, `counter` stays frozen at 0 forever and plain
// `rck` doesn't even touch the IO register, so every chain read back the same stale value and
// always started from the same point. Rather than add the missing `cls` setup call, this
// version drops the clock dependency entirely and uses a small fixed-seed xorshift-style
// generator instead (rndval, below) -- a traditional, simple PRNG: shift, xor, repeat a few
// times. It's seeded once from a fixed constant at cold load (SEED) and then advances by one
// mix step every time a new chain starts, so successive chains land in different places even
// though the whole sequence is deterministic and reproducible run-to-run. Bit 0 of the mixed
// value picks the starting edge (top or left); a few more bits, masked and offset, pick where
// along that edge.
//
// GRAVITY FROM THE FRONT-PANEL TEST SWITCHES
// ---------------------------------------------
// `lat` (0762200) is a single instruction that both clears AC and ORs in the live test-switch
// register (pdp1.c: bit B10 clears AC, bit B7 ORs in pdp->tw, both bits are baked into lat's
// own opcode) -- confirmed against pdp1.c rather than assumed, since guessing at instruction
// semantics is exactly what's bitten this file before. pdp->tw is set straight from the panel's
// live switches (panel1.c: pdp->tw = sw1), so lat is a live snapshot at the instant it runs, not
// something that needs separate arming.
//
// Reread every time a chain restarts (not just once at cold load), so flipping switches mid-run
// changes the very next chain -- no reload needed. 0 means "keep the built-in default"
// (GRAVITY, below); nonzero is used directly as the gravity magnitude in raster units/frame^2,
// masked to the low 8 bits (0-255) only to stay clear of the sign bit, not to limit how extreme
// a setting someone can dial in -- past roughly 100-150 the fall is already down to a couple of
// frames given the screen's height, so the extra headroom above that mostly just lets the high
// end of the dial feel appropriately silly rather than adding a meaningfully different speed.
//
// wje/Claude 9-Jul-2026 - initial version
// wje/Claude 9-Jul-2026 - replaced the (broken) clock-seeded RNG with a fixed-seed xorshift
// wje/Claude 9-Jul-2026 - gravity now reread from the test switches every chain restart
// wje/Claude 9-Jul-2026 - ball is now a proper digitized circle (radius 8) instead of a tiny box;
//                          added RESTY so the bigger ball rests on the floor line, not through it
// wje/Claude 9-Jul-2026 - switched gen_circle.py from the single-octant midpoint/Bresenham
//                          method to dense-sample-and-round: the octant method's long repeated-
//                          diagonal runs read as flat facets ("cube like") at this radius; dense
//                          sampling keeps worst-case deviation under half a screen pixel

#include <TYPE340/type340defs.ah>

#define BALLSCALE 1
#define BALLINTENSITY 6
#define FLOORINTENSITY 2

#define SEED 0d26317      // arbitrary nonzero fixed seed for the xorshift generator

#define GRAVITY 0d2       // default downward accel -- raster units/frame^2 -- switches all down
#define LAUNCHVX 0d5      // constant horizontal drift -- raster units/frame
#define BALLRADIUS 0d8    // ball radius, raster units -- rerun gen_circle.py if this changes
#define FLOORY 0d20       // y coordinate the floor LINE is drawn at
#define RESTY (FLOORY+BALLRADIUS) // y coordinate the ball's CENTER rests/bounces at
#define TOPSTARTY 0d980   // y used for a "drop from the top edge" start
#define LEFTSTARTX 0d20   // x used for a "launch from the left edge" start
#define RIGHTLIMIT 0d1000 // chain ends once the ball drifts past this x
#define MINBOUNCE 0d4     // post-bounce |bvy| below this -> chain has settled

var bx=0, by=0, bvx=0, bvy=0
var rndval=SEED, tmpval=0, dlycnt=0, gravval=GRAVITY

100/
// ---- pick this chain's gravity and start point ----
newchain,
    // gravity: read the front-panel test switches fresh every chain. 0 -> keep the default;
    // nonzero -> use directly (masked to 8 bits, see header comment for why that's not tighter).
    lat
    and [0377]
    sza                      // skip (use the switch value) when AC != 0
    jmp useswitches
    lac [GRAVITY]            // falls through here when AC == 0 -- switches are all down
    dac gravval
    jmp gravdone
useswitches,
    dac gravval
gravdone,

    // advance the fixed-seed xorshift-style generator by one mix step (shift, xor, x3)
    lac rndval
    dac tmpval
    sal 5s
    xor tmpval
    dac rndval
    lac rndval
    dac tmpval
    sar 7s
    xor tmpval
    dac rndval
    lac rndval
    dac tmpval
    sal 3s
    xor tmpval
    dac rndval

    lac rndval
    and [1]                 // bit 0 chooses the starting edge
    sza
    jmp fromleft

fromtop,
    lac rndval
    sar 2s
    and [0777]               // 0-511
    add [0d150]               // -> 150-661, comfortably inside the screen width
    dac bx
    lac [TOPSTARTY]
    dac by
    jmp launch

fromleft,
    lac rndval
    sar 2s
    and [0777]               // 0-511
    add [0d200]               // -> 200-711, comfortably between the floor and the top
    dac by
    lac [LEFTSTARTX]
    dac bx

launch,
    lac [LAUNCHVX]
    dac bvx
    dzm bvy

// ---- physics + redraw, once per frame ----
frame,
    // gravity: constant downward accel (this chain's setting, from the switches or default)
    lac bvy
    sub gravval
    dac bvy

    // integrate position
    lac bx
    add bvx
    dac bx
    lac by
    add bvy
    dac by

    // floor collision -- bounce if the ball's center has reached or passed its rest height
    lac by
    sub [RESTY]
    spa                      // skip (no bounce) when by >= RESTY
    jmp bounce
    jmp aftercollision
bounce,
    lac [RESTY]
    dac by                   // clamp to the rest height (ball's bottom edge on the floor line)
    lac bvy
    cma
    dac bvy                  // reflect: bvy = -bvy (now positive, moving up)
    lac bvy
    sar 2s
    dac tmpval
    lac bvy
    sub tmpval
    dac bvy                  // lose 1/4 of the speed on this bounce (3/4 restitution)

    lac bvy
    sub [MINBOUNCE]
    spa                      // skip (still bouncing) when bvy >= MINBOUNCE
    jmp newchain             // too slow to matter now -- settle, start a new chain
aftercollision,

    // drifted off the right edge -- this chain is done too
    lac bx
    sub [RIGHTLIMIT]
    sma                      // skip (still on screen) when bx < RIGHTLIMIT
    jmp newchain

    // patch the ball's new position into the live display list
    lac by
    and [01777]
    ior [y(0) next(point)]
    dac bally
    lac bx
    and [01777]
    ior [show x(0) next(increment)]
    dac ballx

    lio [prog]
    dla
waitstop,
    dss
    jmp waitstop

    // pacing delay -- tune to taste, more negative = slower animation
    lac [0-0d800]
    dac dlycnt
delayloop,
    isp dlycnt
    jmp delayloop

    jmp frame

variables
constants

// Begins in parameter mode. bally/ballx are placeholders -- always overwritten by the host
// before the first dla ever runs, same convention cube2.am1 uses for tgty/tgtx.
prog,
    scale(BALLSCALE) intensity(BALLINTENSITY) next(point)
bally, y(0d500) next(point)
ballx, show x(0d500) next(increment)
    // invisible walk from the center out to the circle's rightmost point (BALLRADIUS steps)
    set1(incright) set2(incright) set3(incright) set4(incright)
    set1(incright) set2(incright) set3(incright) set4(incright)
    // digitized radius-8 circle boundary, 64 steps, generated by gen_circle.py -- see header
    visible set1(incup) set2(incup) set3(incup) set4(incleft)
    visible set1(incup) set2(incup) set3(incleft) set4(incup)
    visible set1(incleft) set2(incup) set3(incleft) set4(incleft)
    visible set1(incup) set2(incleft) set3(incleft) set4(incleft)
    visible set1(incleft) set2(incleft) set3(incleft) set4(incdown)
    visible set1(incleft) set2(incleft) set3(incdown) set4(incleft)
    visible set1(incdown) set2(incleft) set3(incdown) set4(incdown)
    visible set1(incleft) set2(incdown) set3(incdown) set4(incdown)
    visible set1(incdown) set2(incdown) set3(incdown) set4(incright)
    visible set1(incdown) set2(incdown) set3(incright) set4(incdown)
    visible set1(incright) set2(incdown) set3(incright) set4(incright)
    visible set1(incdown) set2(incright) set3(incright) set4(incright)
    visible set1(incright) set2(incright) set3(incright) set4(incup)
    visible set1(incright) set2(incright) set3(incup) set4(incright)
    visible set1(incup) set2(incright) set3(incup) set4(incup)
    visible set1(incright) set2(incup) set3(incup) set4(incup)
    end

    // static floor line, drawn every frame right along with the ball
    scale(1) intensity(FLOORINTENSITY) next(point)
    y(FLOORY) next(point)
    x(0d0) show next(vcontinue)
    visible right deltax(0d1)

    halt
    halt

start newchain
